Easy win 👍
You just need to save the workbook right after the history log is written, so you’re guaranteed the run data is not lost even if Excel crashes.

Below are safe and clean ways to do it.


---

✅ Option 1: Auto-Save After Logging (Most Common)

🔹 Update your Main Macro

Sub RunMainProcess()

    Call ClearTimeLog
    Call LogStartTime

    ' ===== YOUR MAIN PROCESS =====
    Application.Wait Now + TimeValue("00:00:05")
    ' =============================

    Call LogEndTimeAndDuration
    Call LogRunHistory

    ' Save workbook after logging
    Call SaveAfterLog

End Sub


---

🔹 Save Sub (Simple & Safe)

Sub SaveAfterLog()

    If ThisWorkbook.Saved = False Then
        ThisWorkbook.Save
    End If

End Sub

✔ Saves only if there are changes
✔ No pop-ups
✔ Works for manual or scheduled runs


---

✅ Option 2: Save with Timestamp Backup (Recommended for Audits)

This creates a backup copy before saving.

Sub SaveWithBackup()

    Dim backupPath As String

    backupPath = ThisWorkbook.Path & "\Backup_" & _
                 Format(Now, "yyyymmdd_hhmmss") & ".xlsm"

    ThisWorkbook.SaveCopyAs backupPath
    ThisWorkbook.Save

End Sub

💡 Great if your macro is business-critical.


---

⚠️ Important Edge Case (First-Time Save)

If the file was never saved before, ThisWorkbook.Path is empty. Use this safe version:

Sub SaveAfterLogSafe()

    If ThisWorkbook.Path = "" Then
        MsgBox "Please save the workbook first before running the macro.", vbExclamation
        Exit Sub
    End If

    ThisWorkbook.Save

End Sub


---

🧠 Best Practice (What I Recommend)

For production macros:

Call LogRunHistory
Call SaveAfterLogSafe

This ensures: ✔ Run is logged
✔ File is safely saved
✔ No silent failures


